Q: CS50 speller problem segmentation fault first, then I found it failed to load the dictionary correctly A: <p>In analyzing your issue and the good comments, I did see that you had addressed the issue of having a return statement embedded within your dictionary load function that was effectively only loading one word.  To illustrate this part of the issue, I added a print statement in the main function noting the size of the dictionary.</p>
<pre><code>    if (!loaded)
    {
        printf(&quot;Could not load %s.\n&quot;, dictionary);
        return 1;
    }

    printf(&quot;Number of word(s) loaded: %d\n&quot;, size());
</code></pre>
<p>That, coupled with the for loop within the word checking function produced a condition where undefined memory was being accessed.</p>
<pre><code>bool check(const char *word)
{
    // get hash index
    int index = hash(word);
    if (table[index] == NULL)
        printf(&quot;Word to check: %s  Hash: %d  node value: %p\n&quot;, word, index, table[index]);
    //loop to run thru the linked list and do comparison
    for(node* temp = table[index]; temp-&gt;next!=NULL; temp=temp-&gt;next)
    {
        if (strcasecmp(temp-&gt;word, word) == 0)
        {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}
</code></pre>
<p>FYI, for this testing a very small dictionary was being used.</p>
<pre><code>aid
angel
apple
axis
banana
beach
forest
ibeam
level
men
north
open
parting
pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis
school
tourist
understanding
velocity
versatility
xray
yesterday
zoo
</code></pre>
<p>And, a small set of sample words were being tested for existence in the dictionary.</p>
<pre><code>angel
APPLE
Banana
ibeam
ideal
level
load
north
nope
pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis
quick
school
sky
state
</code></pre>
<p>With that, the initial test of the code was run illustrating that the first table element was initialized with the first word in the dictionary (&quot;aid&quot;), allowing word checks for any words starting with &quot;a&quot;, but then getting a segmentation fault when moving onto the next letter in the alphabet.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/Spell_9/bin/Release$ ./Spell_9 Stuff.txt Tests.txt
Number of word(s) loaded: 1

MISSPELLED WORDS

angel
APPLE
Word to check: Banana  Hash: 1  node value: (nil)
Segmentation fault (core dumped)
</code></pre>
<p>Commenting out that extraneous &quot;return&quot; statement allows for the loading of the dictionary.  However, further testing with a small dictionary revealed additional caveats within the word checking function.</p>
<p>Although unlikely in a dictionary with ample words in it, along with the simple hashing function, the test dictionary illustrates that a segmentation fault could still occur if a hash value for a word being tested resulted in pointing at a hash table with a null address.  In this scenario, there are no entries in the dictionary for the letter &quot;q&quot;, so when the sample word &quot;quiet&quot; is checked, a segmentation fault occurs.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/Spell_9/bin/Release$ ./Spell_9 Stuff.txt Tests.txt
Number of word(s) loaded: 22

MISSPELLED WORDS

ideal
load
nope
Segmentation fault (core dumped)
</code></pre>
<p>Again, for the program's hashing function with a comprehensive dictionary, one might not incur this issue.  However, if a more complex hashing operation were to be used with a larger hash table and the possibility of some table entries containing a null entry, this segmentation fault issue could still crop up.  With that, following is a further refactoring of the function to avoid such a possible scenario.</p>
<pre><code>bool check(const char *word)
{
    // get hash index
    int index = hash(word);

    if (table[index] == NULL)   /* No entry at the hash location - return false */
        return false;
    //loop to run thru the linked list and do comparison
    for(node* temp = table[index]; temp-&gt;next!=NULL; temp=temp-&gt;next)   /* False negative if only one word in hash table element    */
    {
        if (strcasecmp(temp-&gt;word, word) == 0)
        {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you note in the code block, there is a comment about &quot;false negative&quot; conditions.  Unlikely that this might be seen in a larger dictionary; however, if the hash table size was much larger where possibly only one node entry was in a table (e.g. the table[i]-&gt;next node was null) words being checked that were actually in the dictionary could be potentially tagged as misspelled as noted in the following test output.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/Spell_9/bin/Release$ ./Spell_9 Stuff.txt Tests.txt
Number of word(s) loaded: 22

MISSPELLED WORDS

Banana  &lt;--- in the dictionary
ibeam   &lt;--- in the dictionary
ideal
level   &lt;--- in the dictionary
load
north   &lt;--- in the dictionary
nope
quick
school  &lt;--- in the dictionary
sky
state

WORDS MISSPELLED:     11
WORDS IN DICTIONARY:  22
WORDS IN TEXT:        14
</code></pre>
<p>This condition falls back to the initialization and testing of the &quot;temp-&gt;next&quot; value.  Instead of attempting to tweak or refactor this block of code as a for loop, a while loop block was used in the following refactored code.</p>
<pre><code>bool check(const char *word)
{
    // get hash index
    int index = hash(word);

    if (table[index] == NULL)       /* No entry at the hash location - return false */
        return false;
    //loop to run thru the linked list and do comparison
    node * temp = table[index];
    while (true)
    {
        if (strcasecmp(temp-&gt;word, word) == 0)
        {
            return true;
        }
        if (temp-&gt;next == NULL)     /* No more entries to check - exit while loop   */
            break;
        temp = temp-&gt;next;
    }
    return false;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Following was the subsequent test with the terminal output which agrees with words that actually were not found in the dictionary.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/Spell_9/bin/Release$ ./Spell_9 Stuff.txt Tests.txt
Number of word(s) loaded: 22

MISSPELLED WORDS

ideal
load
nope
quick
sky
state

WORDS MISSPELLED:     6
WORDS IN DICTIONARY:  22
WORDS IN TEXT:        14
</code></pre>
<p>The main things to take away from this testing and refactoring are as follows:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Probably delve some more into C tutorials to be more aware of the proper usage of return statements, for loop setup, and while loop usage with appropriate testing and break statements.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Get comfortable with debugging tools or as an alternative, usage of temporary print statements at suspect points in a program to uncover potential issues such as segmentation faults, memory allocation, and memory deallocation.</p>
</li>
</ul>
